<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Pomerania-Stettin</h1><p class="st-meta">1295 CE – 1625 CE (approx.) · capital Szczecin</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The southern Pomeranian duchy created in 1295, centred on the Oder port of Stettin and long contested with Brandenburg.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The partition of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-pomerania">Duchy of Pomerania</a> in 1295 gave Otto I the lands around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/szczecin">Stettin</a> (Szczecin) on the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oder-river">Oder</a>, while his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogislaw-iv-duke-of-pomerania">Bogislaw IV</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania-wolgast">Pomerania-Wolgast</a>. Otto's descendants spent much of the 14th and 15th centuries resisting the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/margraviate-of-brandenburg">Margraviate of Brandenburg</a>'s claims to overlordship and to the succession.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/szczecin">Stettin</a> was a Hanseatic port exporting grain, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> and herring, and its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burghers">burghers</a> often acted independently of the dukes. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogislaw-x">Bogislaw X</a>, who reunited all <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-pomerania">Pomerania</a> in 1478, reformed its administration and finances. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-grimnitz">Treaty of Grimnitz</a> in 1529 freed the dukes from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg's</a> feudal claims in exchange for a promise of succession, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a> adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutheranism">Lutheranism</a> in 1534.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After a new division in 1532, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/szczecin">Stettin</a> was ruled by a series of dukes who died without heirs. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogislaw-xiv">Bogislaw XIV</a> united both duchies in 1625, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> occupied <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-pomerania">Pomerania</a> in 1630. On his death in 1637 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-griffins">Griffin</a> line ended, and in 1648 the lands were divided between <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/swedish-pomerania">Swedish Pomerania</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/duchy-of-pomerania-stettin.jpg" alt="Duchy of Pomerania-Stettin" width="320" height="204"><figcaption>Image: J a1 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grunwald_S%C5%82upsk_i_Szczecin.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1295 CE – 1625 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Szczecin</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Otto I, Bogislaw X, Bogislaw XIV</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism, then Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Low German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a>, <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Pommern-Stettin</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1460&amp;focus=duchy-of-pomerania-stettin" aria-label="Duchy of Pomerania-Stettin on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5004 341 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duchy of Pomerania-Stettin lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5324 597L5324 597"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5324 597h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5324 597h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5324 597h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Szczecin. <a href="/atlas?year=1460&amp;focus=duchy-of-pomerania-stettin">On 